max-visits: 8
on-arrival: f_ev_last = time

= Los que quieren entrar

They come to the camp in twos and threes — a boy whose brother the army took, a girl who can already outshoot half the column, a campesino who has decided that a machete against a G-3 is still better than kneeling. Recruits are the only reinforcement a foco gets, and they are also mouths, and risk, and the endless problem Che named: an army that grows faster than it can train or feed is an army that dies of its own weight.

- @reclutas_tomar: Take them all. Numbers now — the war needs bodies and the villages are offering them. *(+hombres; strains comida; a little green)*
- @reclutas_cuidar: Take only who the base vouches for by blood, and train them right before they carry a rifle. *(fewer, steadier; +discipline)*

@reclutas_tomar
on-arrival: {! Q.f_hombres+=6; Q.f_comida-=6; Q.f_moral-=2; Q.apoyo_popular+=2; Q.f_reclutas=(Q.f_reclutas||0)+6; !}

The column swells. There are more rifles in the line and more stories of why, and the villages see their sons standing in the ranks and love you for taking them. There are also more mouths on a supply that was already thin, and greener hands on the triggers, and the veterans trade a look that says *we will pay for this the first time the shooting is serious.* Both things are true. They usually are.

- @root: Continuar.

@reclutas_cuidar
on-arrival: {! Q.f_hombres+=2; Q.disciplina=(Q.disciplina||65)+4; Q.f_moral+=2; Q.f_reclutas=(Q.f_reclutas||0)+2; !}

You take the two the base can vouch for by blood and turn the rest back with a promise and a task — organize your village, wait, we will come. It costs you the numbers. It buys you a column that will not break the first bad afternoon, and a bench of trained people in every village against the day you need them. Bayo's rule, and Che's before the end: a guerrilla's discipline is mostly the discipline of not growing faster than you can hold.

- @root: Continuar.

